---
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";
import MethodologyNav from "../../components/MethodologyNav.astro";
import MethodFacts from "../../components/MethodFacts.astro";
import { M, axisByKey, percent, stackingPercents, stackingTailPercent } from "../../lib/methodology";

const axis = axisByKey("code_safety");
const cap = (s: string) => s[0].toUpperCase() + s.slice(1);
const rows = M.code_safety_deductions;
const crit = rows[0];
// `03` §3's worked example, computed from the table rather than typed: two
// critical findings, the second at the second rung of the ladder.
const second = (crit.medium as number) * Number(M.stacking[1]);
const RULES = "https://github.com/kVadrum/mcpwatchman/tree/main/rules";
---

<Doc
  title="Code Safety — mcpwatchman methodology"
  description="How mcpwatchman's Code Safety axis scores MCP servers: an MCP-specific semgrep ruleset, the deduction table by severity and confidence, and the stacking rule."
  canonical="https://mcpwatchman.com/methodology/code-safety/"
  heading="Code Safety"
  crumb={{ href: "/methodology/", label: "Methodology" }}
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      Code patterns that produce a security impact if attacker-controlled
      input reaches them: shell execution, server-side request forgery,
      unsafe deserialization, path traversal. Provisional weight{" "}
      {percent(axis.weight)}%.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
  <MethodFacts slot="facts" />

  <MethodologyNav />

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="reads-heading">
    <h2 id="reads-heading">What is read</h2>
    <p>
      An MCP-specific semgrep ruleset runs over the server's published source
      in Python, JavaScript and TypeScript, and Go. The categories are shell
      execution, SSRF, deserialization and path traversal in every language,
      SQL injection in Python and prototype pollution in JavaScript, plus rules
      keyed on the shape of an MCP tool handler. A tool argument is a value a
      language model was persuaded to produce, so “the caller is trusted” is
      not a mitigation. Every rule is public in the{" "}
      <a href={RULES}>repository's rules directory</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rules are patterns, not taint analysis: they do not follow data
      across functions, which is why no confidence above medium is claimed for
      them.
    </p>
    <p>
      A server is graded on its own code. Vendored dependencies are not read,
      nor is build output (<code>dist/</code>, <code>build/</code>), because a
      bundle inlines other people's code; nor are minified files. When a
      package ships only build output, Code Safety is scored on the
      repository it declares, and the axis says so. A scanned repository's own
      scanner configuration is removed before the scan, so a repository cannot
      exclude itself from it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="paper doc-paper" aria-labelledby="table-heading">
    <h2 id="table-heading">Deductions</h2>
    <p>
      The axis opens at 100, each finding deducts by its severity and its
      confidence, and the axis floors at 0. Low-confidence findings deduct at
      the lowest value in their severity's row.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Severity</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">High confidence</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Medium confidence</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) =>
            "any" in r ? (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{cap(r.severity)}</th>
                <td class="num deduct" colspan="2">{r.any === 0 ? "0, listed only" : `−${r.any} at any confidence`}</td>
              </tr>
            ) : (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{cap(r.severity)}</th>
                <td class="num dim">−{r.high}</td>
                <td class="num deduct">−{r.medium}</td>
              </tr>
            ),
          )}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The high-confidence column is greyed because it is unused: no rule may
      claim high confidence until the gold set measures its false-positive
      rate, so every finding is capped at {M.confidence_ceiling}.
    </p>

    <h3>Stacking</h3>
    <p>
      Findings of one severity stack with diminishing returns. Within a
      severity, the largest deduction is taken first at {stackingPercents[0]}%,
      then the rest at {stackingPercents.slice(1).join("%, ")}% and{" "}
      {stackingTailPercent}% from the fifth on. Grouping is by severity alone, so
      a critical/high and a critical/medium finding share a group. Only the
      final axis score is rounded, half up: two critical findings at medium
      confidence deduct {crit.medium} + {second} = {(crit.medium as number) + second},
      so the axis is {100 - ((crit.medium as number) + second)}.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="severity-heading">
    <h2 id="severity-heading">Severity and confidence</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Critical</strong> — a direct, unauthenticated remote impact path, such as shell injection through a tool argument with no validation.</li>
      <li><strong>High</strong> — impact with modest preconditions, such as SSRF restricted to outbound HTTP, or <code>pickle.loads</code> on caller-supplied data.</li>
      <li><strong>Medium</strong> — meaningful preconditions, or bounded impact.</li>
      <li><strong>Low</strong> — a smell with security implications and no clear exploitation path.</li>
      <li><strong>Informational</strong> — recorded, never deducted.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Confidence is a measured false-positive rate, not a feeling: high means
      under 5% on the gold set, medium under 20%, low is a heuristic used only
      where the alternative is missing a class entirely. Until the gold set
      exists, the ceiling is enforced in code.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="evidence-heading">
    <h2 id="evidence-heading">Evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Each finding records the file, line, rule ID, severity, confidence and a
      short excerpt of the matched code, and the server page prints it. A
      finding in test code (<code>tests/</code>, <code>*.test.ts</code>,{" "}
      <code>*_test.go</code> and the like) is listed and marked, and deducts
      nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      An axis is unassessed — never 0 — when there is no source in a language
      the ruleset covers, or no source could be read at all.
    </p>
  </section>
</Doc>
